{"tag":"8PRV","kind":"problem","subkind":"problem","paper":"2605.23846","version":"1","local":"que-1","file":"","label":"basic","number":"1","section":"sec-1","order":1,"title":"Question","body_md":"\\label{basic}\r\nDoes $T$ have a nontrivial hyperinvariant subspace, that is a subspace ${\\cal M}\\in \\hbox{\\rm Lat}({\\cal H}_1\\oplus{\\cal H}_2)$, different from $\\{0\\}\\oplus\\{0\\}$ and ${\\cal H}_1\\oplus{\\cal H}_2$, which is invariant for every $Q\\in\\{T\\}' :\\, Q{\\cal M}\\subset{\\cal M}$?","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"que","item":"problem","head":"environment","counter":"pro","number_from":"render","labels":["basic"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Adjacent_cross-sections_of_the_commutant.tex","from":153,"to":156},"hash":"sha256:99d75d6d99e8c580713a75110a9aee5f2016e38c99525b7cc4af14f9cefc3f2a","minhash":"","census":"2605.23846v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"806W","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2605.23846","version":"1","local":"lem-1","file":"","label":"composition","number":"2","section":"sec-2","order":2,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{composition}\r\nWe have $\\; C_{{\\cal E},{\\cal F}} \\circ C_{{\\cal H},{\\cal E}} = C_{{\\cal H},{\\cal F}}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"pro","number_from":"render","labels":["composition"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Adjacent_cross-sections_of_the_commutant.tex","from":213,"to":216},"hash":"sha256:b52bb9171941dedacf76d43f4d02aec4ea93c573d52f59e7a5633e0d764cfd7c","minhash":"","census":"2605.23846v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"2N6P","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2605.23846","version":"1","local":"lem-2","file":"","label":"partial","number":"3","section":"sec-2","order":3,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{partial}\r\nFor every $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$, we have\r\n\\[P_{1,1}\\circ M_{3,r}\\circ C_{{\\cal H},{\\cal E}_{3,r}}= M_{2,r+1}\\circ C_{{\\cal H},{\\cal F}_{2,r+1}}= P_{3,3}\\circ M_{3,r+1}\\circ C_{{\\cal H},{\\cal E}_{3,r+1}}.\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lem","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"pro","number_from":"render","labels":["partial"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Adjacent_cross-sections_of_the_commutant.tex","from":238,"to":242},"hash":"sha256:b2741bb530e302794ebf23cd5398566712daa093f2ee3818dd7ed124dc797774","minhash":"","census":"2605.23846v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"4MAJ","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2605.23846","version":"1","local":"thm-1","file":"","label":"connection","number":"4","section":"sec-3","order":4,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{connection}\r\nFor every $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$, we have\r\n\\begin{itemize}\r\n\\item[\\textup{(i)}] $\\; q_{3,r}= q_{2,r} q_{2,r+1} \\rho(C^{r+1}_{3,1}),$\r\n\\item[\\textup{(ii)}] $\\; p_{1,r+1}= p_{2,r}p_{2,r+1} \\rho(C^r_{1,1}).$\r\n\\end{itemize}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"thm","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"pro","number_from":"render","labels":["connection"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Adjacent_cross-sections_of_the_commutant.tex","from":355,"to":362},"hash":"sha256:b9f5be1c7e4f650b38c7670be0e04adb99d4d034c714fcf084a37afc6de0a03d","minhash":"","census":"2605.23846v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"1OF6","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2605.23846","version":"1","local":"thm-2","file":"","label":"exclusion","number":"5","section":"sec-4","order":5,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{exclusion}\r\nIf $\\widehat{\\cal L}$ is $D$-normal, then $M_{3,r}(\\widehat{\\cal L}_r)$ is of type {\\rm (T2)}, for every $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"thm","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"pro","number_from":"render","labels":["exclusion"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Adjacent_cross-sections_of_the_commutant.tex","from":491,"to":494},"hash":"sha256:196a551812665d2cb1058877df389c37ef7dc54472ee50c13125357ece65eb7f","minhash":"","census":"2605.23846v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"CGS3","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2605.23846","version":"1","local":"thm-3","file":"","label":"main","number":"6","section":"sec-5","order":6,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{main}\r\nThe linear manifold $\\widehat{\\cal L}$ is strongly $D$-normal, which means that the subspace $M_{3,r}(\\widehat{\\cal L}_r)$ is strongly $\\Delta_r$-normal for every $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$, with parameters $x_r, y_r, q_r\\in\\mathbb{C}_0$.\r\nFurthermore, we have\r\n\\[x_{r+1}= \\frac{y_r}{x_r} \\quad (r\\in\\mathbb{N}).\\]","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"thm","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"pro","number_from":"render","labels":["main"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Adjacent_cross-sections_of_the_commutant.tex","from":675,"to":680},"hash":"sha256:c833f39dd0cf47cd5a42119553d3336a2aceba4a782e6ecc0c69a7a7a9e98742","minhash":"","census":"2605.23846v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
